// Normative rule count_overflow_interrupt (src/priv/sscofpmf.adoc):
//   If an hpmcounter overflows while the associated OF bit is zero, then a "count overflow
//     interrupt request" is generated. If the OF bit is one, then no interrupt request is
//     generated. Consequently the OF bit also functions as a count overflow interrupt disable for
//     the associated hpmcounter.
//
// Context:
//   The request is LCOFI, bit 13 of mip/sip. mideleg[13] is WARL: "An implementation can choose to
//     subset the delegatable traps" (norm:medeleg_mideleg_warl), and bits for lower-level
//     interrupts may even be read-only one (norm:mideleg_rd1_lower_level). S-mode cannot read
//     mideleg, so the test observes it: an M-mode write of mie.LCOFIE shows in sie iff LCOFI is
//     delegated (sip/sie bits of undelegated interrupts are read-only zero). LCOFIP is then read in
//     sip when delegated and in mip (T-SBI) otherwise, LCOFIE is set in mie (T-SBI), and an LCOFI
//     is expected in S/HS-mode when delegated and in M-mode otherwise. Counting uses
//     RVMODEL_HPM_COUNTER with event RVMODEL_MHPMEVENT_VAL, made to count in the current mode by
//     RVMODEL_MHPMEVENT_CODE. The counter is preset with every xINH bit set, and counting is
//     enabled only in the mode under test (M-mode stays inhibited, so the T-SBI handler is not
//     counted). Only whether the counter moved or wrapped is checked, never by how much, so events
//     that count every instruction or cycle also work. These checks exist only when
//     RVMODEL_MHPMEVENT_VAL != 0 (a DUT that can count). Software writes of OF (issue 1981) are not
